    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit rule on `POST /api/owners`: once 100+ owners have been registered today (by `registrationDate`), the create is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests**, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\nNew/changed files:\n\n- **`CheckDailyOwnerLimit.java`** (function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today; throws the checked `DailyOwnerLimitException` at the 100-owner threshold. Runs before `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner`, so nothing is persisted on rejection.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitException.java`** \u2014 checked escalation exception.\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `429` with an `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]` body, mirroring the existing `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` shape.\n- **`...DailyOwnerLimitException.yml`** (escalation wiring) \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `checkDailyLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) between `checkCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nI placed the check inside the existing transaction-governed prelude and before any entity is built, consistent with the other `Check*` guard steps (city capacity, unique telephone/household).",
